---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Button from "../components/ui/Button.astro";

const sections = [
  {
    id: "systems-problem",
    title: "Performance is a systems problem",
    body: [
      "The default explanation for underperformance is personal — someone didn't try hard enough, wasn't disciplined enough. It's a satisfying story because the fix stays entirely in one person's hands. It's also usually incomplete.",
      'Most performance failure — in an athlete, a founder, a team, or a piece of software — traces back to an unclear constraint, an undocumented dependency, or a process that only worked because one person was holding it together by hand. The useful diagnostic question isn\'t "why didn\'t they try harder," it\'s "what did this depend on that nobody wrote down?"',
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "ownership-beats-dependency",
    title: "Ownership beats dependency",
    body: [
      "Rented tools and platforms are the correct call when speed matters more than durability. The problem shows up later, quietly, when the rented thing becomes load-bearing — and a pricing change, policy shift, or platform sunset turns out to have been sitting underneath the whole business.",
      "Ownership doesn't mean building everything from scratch. It means knowing which parts of a system are core enough that renting them is a real risk — domain, content, customer data, the ability to move if you have to — and owning those specifically, while everything replaceable stays rented.",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "load-path",
    title: "Build for the load path, not the launch day",
    body: [
      "In structural engineering, the load path is the route a force takes from where it's applied down to the foundation. A structure that looks finished but has no coherent load path doesn't fail on day one — it fails under real load, later, when it matters most.",
      "The same is true of a brand, a website, or an operating process. Launch day is the easy case: controlled, rehearsed, low load. The real test is what happens under growth, change, or pressure — a viral moment, a team handoff, a platform outage. Systems here are built for that case, not for the demo.",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "documentation",
    title: "Documentation is an operating advantage",
    body: [
      "Undocumented systems have a single point of failure: the person who remembers how they work. That's not a moral failing, it's an architecture problem, and it's fixable.",
      "Documentation isn't overhead here — it's what lets a system be handed off, audited, improved, or run by someone other than its builder. A system that can't be explained on paper usually can't be trusted to run without its builder in the room.",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "culture-is-infrastructure",
    title: "Culture is infrastructure",
    body: [
      "The habits and standards a team defaults to under pressure are as load-bearing as any piece of software. A well-documented process still fails if the operating culture doesn't reinforce using it.",
      "Treating culture as infrastructure means designing it deliberately — what gets rewarded, what gets checked, what the default behavior is when no one's watching — instead of hoping it emerges on its own.",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "technology-decision",
    title: "Technology must produce a real decision or action",
    body: [
      "A dashboard nobody acts on, an AI workflow that outputs something no one uses, an automation that runs and reports to no one — these are technically functioning and operationally worthless.",
      "The bar for any system built here is that it changes what someone actually decides or does next. If it doesn't produce a decision or an action, it's not finished — it's decoration.",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "human-agency",
    title: "The best systems preserve human agency",
    body: [
      "Automation and AI are tools for removing repetitive load from a system, not for removing the human's ability to see what's happening and intervene. A system that quietly makes all the decisions is fragile in a different way than an undocumented one — it's opaque.",
      "The goal is a system that does the repeatable work reliably and leaves the judgment calls, the exceptions, and the final say with the person accountable for the outcome.",
    ],
  },
];

const breadcrumbLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
  itemListElement: [
    { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://alexandermathai.com/" },
    {
      "@type": "ListItem",
      position: 2,
      name: "Operating Thesis",
      item: "https://alexandermathai.com/thesis",
    },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Operating Thesis — Alexander Mathai"
  description="First-principles essays on how systems fail, how ownership beats dependency, and why documentation, culture, and human agency are load-bearing parts of any durable operation."
  structuredData={[breadcrumbLd]}
>
  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Operating Thesis</p>
      <h1>The method behind the ecosystem.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        These are the first-principles ideas that shape how every system here gets diagnosed, built,
        and documented — across consulting, media, product, and performance work.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section thesis-diagram" aria-labelledby="load-path-diagram-heading">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 id="load-path-diagram-heading" class="visually-hidden">Load path diagram</h2>
      <div class="thesis-diagram__figure">
        <svg
          viewBox="0 0 800 220"
          role="img"
          aria-labelledby="load-path-diagram-desc"
          xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
        >
          <desc id="load-path-diagram-desc">
            A diagram showing force applied at the top ("ambition") traveling down through four
            labeled points — architecture, infrastructure, documentation, foundation — to the
            ground, illustrating that every applied force needs a continuous, engineered path to
            bear it.
          </desc>
          <line
            x1="400"
            y1="20"
            x2="400"
            y2="190"
            stroke="var(--color-border-strong)"
            stroke-width="1"
            stroke-dasharray="4 4"></line>
          <path
            d="M400 30 L400 70 L300 110 L300 150 L400 190"
            fill="none"
            stroke="var(--color-text-muted)"
            stroke-width="3"
            stroke-linecap="round"
            stroke-linejoin="round"></path>
          <circle cx="400" cy="30" r="6" fill="var(--color-text-muted)"></circle>
          <circle cx="300" cy="110" r="6" fill="var(--color-text-muted)"></circle>
          <circle cx="400" cy="190" r="6" fill="var(--color-text-muted)"></circle>
          <rect
            x="120"
            y="190"
            width="560"
            height="14"
            fill="var(--color-graphite)"
            stroke="var(--color-border-strong)"></rect>
          <g font-family="ui-monospace, monospace" font-size="12" fill="var(--color-text-faint)">
            <text x="410" y="18">AMBITION (FORCE APPLIED)</text>
            <text x="220" y="105">ARCHITECTURE + DOCUMENTATION</text>
            <text x="290" y="215">FOUNDATION</text>
          </g>
        </svg>
        <p class="label-mono thesis-diagram__caption">
          A force with no continuous path to the foundation doesn't fail on day one — it fails under
          real load.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="thesis-sections">
    {
      sections.map((section) => (
        <section class="section section--tight" id={section.id}>
          <div class="wrap prose">
            <h2>{section.title}</h2>
            {section.body.map((p) => (
              <p>{p}</p>
            ))}
          </div>
        </section>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="wrap thesis-cta">
      <div>
        <h2>See the method applied</h2>
        <p class="lede">Selected system briefs show how these principles show up in actual work.</p>
      </div>
      <Button href="/work" variant="primary">See the work</Button>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .thesis-diagram {
    background: var(--color-bg-raised);
  }

  .thesis-diagram__figure {
    max-width: 44rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .thesis-diagram__figure svg {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }

  .thesis-diagram__caption {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-2);
  }

  .thesis-sections .prose p + p {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-2);
  }

  .thesis-sections .prose h2 {
    margin-block-end: var(--space-2);
  }

  .thesis-cta {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-3);
  }
</style>
